            "creation_time": "2026-06-15T19:32:39Z",
            "text": "Just looked at this again and noticed that I switched the examples around, it should read as:\n\n - `body()` retains srcref attributes for braced functions, but not unbraced ones:\n\n\n```\n      options(keep.source=TRUE) # force for batch sessions\n      is.null(attributes(body(function(x) x)))\n      # [1] TRUE\n      is.null(attributes(body(function(x) {x} )))\n      # [1] FALSE\n```\n\n - `identical(ignore.srcref=TRUE)` does not apply to the `body()` of a closure, only the closure itself:\n\n```\n      options(keep.source=TRUE)\n      f1 = function(x) { x }\n      f2 = function(x) {\n        x\n      }\n      identical(f1, f2)\n      # [1] TRUE\n      identical(body(f1), body(f2))\n      # [1] FALSE\n```",
            "bug_id": 19083,
            "count": 1
          },
          {
            "count": 2,
            "is_markdown": true,
            "text": "Note that \"omit braces around a 1-line function body\"\nis recommended by humans who prefer \"beauty\" and \"cleanness\" over  robotic rules and \"coding standards\".   \nYes it seems these humans are dying out in today's world, really sad in my view.\n\nAfter all that: Yes, of course, this is still a bug which should be addressed!\n(hence `CONFIRMED`).",
            "bug_id": 19083,
            "creator": "maechler",
            "creation_time": "2026-06-16T09:14:34Z",
            "is_private": false,
            "time": "2026-06-16T09:14:34Z",
            "id": 103207,
            "tags": [],
            "attachment_id": {}
          },
          {
            "is_private": false,
            "time": "2026-06-19T21:45:35Z",
            "id": 103220,
            "tags": [],
            "attachment_id": {},
            "count": 3,
            "is_markdown": true,
            "text": "After\n\n```\nF <- function (.) { standardGeneric(\"G\") }\nsetGeneric(\"G\", F)\n```\n\nI think that `body(F)` and `body(G)` should be identical, as currently.  Stripping braces, so that `body(G)` becomes `body(F)[[2L]]`, would violate the expectation of users accustomed to parsing of calls to `function`, including ones like `function (.) { UseMethod(\"G\") }`, which keeps braces.\n\nA smaller change would be to arrange for `setGeneric` to do `new(\"standardGeneric\", F, ...)` instead of `new(\"nonstandardGenericFunction\", F, ...)`.  Something like:\n\n```\nIndex: src/library/methods/R/RMethodUtils.R\n===================================================================\n--- src/library/methods/R/RMethodUtils.R\t(revision 90180)\n+++ src/library/methods/R/RMethodUtils.R\t(working copy)\n@@ -1215,7 +1215,10 @@\n \n .NonstandardGenericTest <- function(body, fname, stdBody)\n {\n-    if(identical(body, stdBody))\n+    if(identical(body, stdBody) ||\n+       (is.call(body) && length(body) == 2L &&\n+        identical(body[[1L]], quote(`{`)) &&\n+        identical(body[[2L]], stdBody)))\n         FALSE\n     else if(.recursiveCallTest(body, fname))\n         TRUE\n```\n\nThen, compared to current behaviour, `body(G)` is unchanged (still a call to `{`) but `class(G)` is `\"standardGeneric\"` instead of `\"nonstandardGenericFunction\"`.\n\nHowever, from reading and testing, I notice that nowhere in R is the standard-nonstandard distinction actually used.  In particular, it seems possible to *remove* from **methods** these (documented as internal) class definitions\n\n```\nstandardGeneric\nstandardGenericWithTrace\nnonstandardGeneric\nnonstandardGenericWithTrace\nnonstandardGenericFunction\nnonstandardGroupGenericFunction\n```\n\n*keeping* in **methods** just these ones\n\n```\ngenericFunction\ngenericFunctionWithTrace\ngroupGenericFunction\ngroupGenericFunctionWithTrace\n```\n\nwithout changing documented behaviour.  Unfortunately, a small number of packages (15-20 across CRAN and BioC, according to a static analysis) have relied inappropriately on **methods** internals by, e.g., testing `is(., \"standardGeneric\")` where `is(., \"genericFunction\")` would have sufficed.\n\nHence I may propose a staged removal within R-core, leaving time between now and the release of R 4.7 to provide package maintainers with patches for their code.  Fixing this \"bug\" with such a simplification, rather than a further complication of `setGeneric` logic, would be nice.  Well, it's just a proposal for now and I'll want to explore more ...",
            "bug_id": 19083,
            "creator": "jaganmn2",
            "creation_time": "2026-06-19T21:45:35Z"
          },
